      "content": "I can help you design a more robust reorder point model to tackle those errant lead times. You can consider implementing a model that incorporates a time-varying lead time, which is often referred to as a \"lead time distribution\" or \"LT distribution.\" This approach can help you better capture the variability in lead times, especially during high-demand periods.\n\nOne popular method for modeling LT distributions is the \"Exponential Smoothing\" (ES) approach. This method uses a weighted average of past lead times to forecast future lead times. The weights are adjusted based on the volatility of the lead time data, which can be achieved by incorporating a measure of lead time variability, such as the coefficient of variation (CV).\n\nFor example, you can use the following formula to estimate the LT distribution:\n\nLT_t = (1 - α) * LT_t-1 + α * LT_t\n\nwhere LT_t is the current lead time, α is the smoothing parameter, and LT_t-1 is the previous lead time.\n\nTo account for the anomalies in lead times, you can also consider using a \"robust\" or \"outlier-resistant\" method, such as the \"Median Absolute Deviation\" (MAD) or the \"Interquartile",
